     "introduction": {
    "title": "Getting to the Start",
    "text": "Getting to the starting point of the Pahi Coastal Walk, right at the tip of the Coromandel Peninsula at Port Jackson, is a bit of a logistical challenge, particularly if you don't have your own transport. Luckily, we had driven there ourselves, making the journey a little easier. \nWe headed to Colville, where we met up with our walking companions, the Scenic Walkers group. We knew everyone from our previous adventures, so it was more like catching up with old friends than meeting a group of fellow walkers for the first time.\nWe spent the night at the Hike and Bike outfit in Colville, which provided a convenient base before the big day. The following morning, we were transferred by minibus to Port Jackson, where the walk was due to begin.\nBefore setting off, we enjoyed a welcome morning tea, giving everyone a chance to chat, take in the surroundings and get into the spirit of the adventure. We were also briefed on the walk ahead, including what to expect along the way.\nWith the introductions, refreshments and briefing all taken care of, it was finally time to lace up our boots and begin the Pahi Coastal Walk!",
    "image": "/kawakawa/kks_gallery",
    "image_alt": "Image from Kawakawa Station."
  },
    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": "Day 01",
        "day_title": "Homestead to Pahi Retreat",
        "day_date": "24-Oct-2026",
         "day_comment": "Our first day on the Pahi Coastal Walk started with a steady uphill climb from the Homestead. It didn't take long before the hard work began to pay off, with some fantastic views opening up across the **Hauraki Gulf (Tikapa Moana)**. On a clear day like ours, we could see both **Te Hauturu-o-Toi (Little Barrier Island)** and **Aotea (Great Barrier Island)** in the distance. Not a bad reward for all that climbing! \nThe real challenge began after passing Pahi Junction, with a long, demanding ascent towards **Barlow's Peak**. At 580 metres, the lookout point provided a perfect excuse to stop, catch our breath and admire the scenery. We were lucky with the weather, and the clear skies allowed us to enjoy magnificent views over the surrounding farmland and rolling Coromandel landscape. \nOf course, what goes up must come down! The descent was long and, in places, seriously steep. By the time we reached the bottom, our knees were certainly feeling the punishment. I suspect they were wondering what they had done to deserve this adventure! \n Fortunately, the day ended on a much more comfortable note. Cold drinks, a cosy cabin, a hot shower and a nice dinner were exactly what we needed. After a challenging but rewarding first day, we couldn't complain.",
        "day_link": [
          {
            "link": "",
            "link_title": "5:30 Hrs",
            "link_icon": "ClockIcon"
          },
          {
            "link": "",
            "link_title": "10.30 kms",
            "link_icon": "ForwardIcon"
          }
        ],
        "day_image": {
          "image": "/kawakawa/kks_day_1",
          "image_number": 1,
          "image_alt": "Image from Pahi Coastal Walk"
        },
        "page": "Odd"
      },
      {
        "day_number": "Day 02",
        "day_title": "Jakeb's Cabins to Purple Hut",
        "day_date": "24-Oct-2020",
        "day_comment": "Day two started with another little test of our determination: a long, 4 km uphill climb to **Saddle View**. By now, our legs were beginning to realise that this was no leisurely coastal stroll! Still, the views made the effort worthwhile. What goes up must come down, of course, and this time we were rewarded with a rather steep descent to **Shepherd's Hut**. \nFrom there, the track continued past **Maloney's Beach** before we eventually reached **Fletcher Bay**, where we stopped for a well-deserved lunch. It was a good opportunity to recharge the batteries and enjoy a bit of a rest before tackling the afternoon section. \nAfter lunch, things became a little easier. We followed the gravel road, occasionally disappearing into clouds of dust raised by passing cars. Not particularly glamorous, but certainly preferable to scrambling across more farmland! Eventually, we reached the beginning of the **Muriwai Walk**. \nJust when we thought the hard work was behind us, an unexpected flight of steep stairs appeared, taking us down to the base of **Wharekaiatua Pā**. A final downhill section brought us to the beach, followed by a long, pleasant stroll along the sand towards our destination. And just like that, another adventure was in the bag! \nThe evening was all about rewarding ourselves: a shower, a relaxing spa, a few drinks and a nice dinner. We'd certainly earned it! \nThe following day, we decided to take things easy. No hills, no steep descents and definitely no stairs! Instead, we enjoyed a relaxed morning, plenty of conversation and good company before being transferred back to Colville for a farewell lunch.",
